What:           /sys/class/mi_display/disp-DSI-<n>/disp_count
Date:           November 2026
Contact:        Xiaomi Venus kernel maintainers
Description:
                Restores the stock Venus display-count persistence interface.
                A read returns these unsigned decimal key=value lines in order:

                  panel_active
                  panel_kickoff_count
                  kernel_boottime
                  kernel_rtctime
                  kernel_days
                  bl_duration
                  bl_level_integral
                  bl_highlevel_duration
                  bl_lowlevel_duration
                  hbm_duration
                  hbm_times
                  fps60_times
                  fps90_times
                  fps120_times
                  record_end

                panel_active is normal-on time in kernel jiffies (HZ=250 in
                the Venus configuration). Successful LP1/LP2 transitions stop
                it; successful ON/NOLP or continuous-splash handoff start it.
                The stock FOD skip-NOLP path also starts the normal-on timer.
                panel_kickoff_count counts non-error DSI encoder submissions,
                including AOD submissions, not completed frames or vblanks.

                bl_duration, bl_highlevel_duration and bl_lowlevel_duration
                are jiffies with nonzero DCS backlight, at least 1536, and
                below 1536 respectively. bl_level_integral is backlight level
                multiplied by jiffies. Only successful DCS brightness writes
                change the recorded level. LP entry does not imply brightness
                zero; panel disable stops all ongoing backlight accounting.

                hbm_duration and fps{60,90,120}_times are seconds. The HBM
                input is the stock brightness_clone value after clamping,
                compared strictly above mi,panel-hbm-backlight-threshold;
                it is not an inferred FOD command or measured luminance.
                hbm_times counts completed nonzero-length HBM intervals.
                HBM seconds round down per interval as in the stock driver;
                reads preserve fractions within an ongoing interval. FPS
                seconds preserve fractions per rate across transitions and
                only accrue while normal-on. Unsupported refresh rates have
                no FPS bucket. Panel disable also ends an HBM interval.

                kernel_boottime is the restored prior-boot total plus current
                boot time, in seconds including suspend. kernel_rtctime is a
                Unix-seconds anchor for kernel_days. Whole days advance the
                anchor. Unset RTC, rollback or a jump beyond ten years reset
                the anchor without inventing elapsed days. record_end is zero.

                A write atomically replaces the persisted totals with all 15
                lines in exactly this order. The final newline is optional.
                Missing/extra fields, signed values, nondecimal values,
                embedded NUL, trailing data and nonzero record_end return
                EINVAL. Decimal overflow returns ERANGE. Writes must be less
                than PAGE_SIZE. Invalid writes leave the state unchanged.
                The live panel state is retained; its elapsed-time baseline
                is reset at restore. This is a prior-boot restore operation,
                not an idempotent writeback of a current-boot read, because
                kernel_boottime is a cumulative prior-boot base on write.

                Counters saturate at U64_MAX. Reads account elapsed intervals
                before taking one coherent snapshot. State is per panel and
                serialized by a leaf spinlock, with no device I/O, allocation,
                display-lock acquisition or formatting inside that lock.
                State is initialized before sysfs attachment and lives inside
                the panel; sysfs removal drains callbacks before panel release.

                Permissions are 0664. The existing vendor early-boot init rule
                assigns the system owner/group. The vendor displaycount daemon
                persists this raw text via pread/pwrite. This interface does
                not implement newer display ioctl 0x0d or feature IDs 35/39/40.
Users:          /vendor/bin/displaycount
